  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This specimen is mainly a thin transport envelope with a clear dispatch boundary, not a routing design. It can carry sparse-knowledge routing payloads, but it does not itself explain multi-hop path discovery, acceptable-path choice, or trustworthy provenance, and the unsigned v0 base makes that especially risky.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear envelope versus payload boundary keeps routing semantics out of hidden wire-format assumptions.",
      "Minimal DAG-CBOR positional encoding is straightforward to implement and can carry bytes without requiring full-topology knowledge at the envelope layer.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling requires unsupported and unverified labeling instead of silent interpretation."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Provides no native multi-hop discovery, path ranking, withdrawal, or loop-avoidance behavior.",
      "Unsigned base envelope offers no built-in authorship, integrity, or peer-local promise accounting for route claims.",
      "Draft status with no frozen or minted pCID weakens 100-year durability evidence."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Sparse-knowledge peers could accept spoofed or replayed route data if payload or transport protections differ.",
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown payloads may encourage unsafe guesses about unsupported routing objects.",
      "Security and audit behavior may fragment across deployments if integrity is left to whatever higher layer happens to carry the envelope."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What payload protocol would carry route advertisements, withdrawals, and acceptable-path evidence?",
      "How should relays add locally auditable hop evidence when this envelope variant is unsigned?",
      "Why does the layering text mention signature slots in an unsigned variant, and what mechanism is intended instead?"
    ],
